<!-- ======================= GLOSSARY & READING ============================= -->
<section class="wrap">
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="frame reveal">
      <h2>Glossary</h2>
      <dl class="glossary">
        <dt>Ecliptic</dt>
        <dd>The apparent annual path of the Sun across the sky — the plane of Earth's orbit, seen from inside it.</dd>
        <dt>Tropical zodiac</dt>
        <dd>Signs measured from the spring equinox, anchored to the seasons. Standard in the West.</dd>
        <dt>Sidereal zodiac</dt>
        <dd>Signs measured against the actual constellations. Standard in Vedic astrology. Currently about 24° apart from the tropical.</dd>
        <dt>Ascendant</dt>
        <dd>The degree of the ecliptic rising on the eastern horizon at the moment of birth. Changes roughly every two hours, which is why birth time matters.</dd>
        <dt>Aspect</dt>
        <dd>An angular relationship between two bodies — the geometric core of chart interpretation.</dd>
        <dt>Orb</dt>
        <dd>The tolerance allowed around an exact aspect. Generous orbs are where a great deal of unfalsifiability enters.</dd>
        <dt>Transit</dt>
        <dd>A current planetary position forming an aspect to a birth chart position.</dd>
        <dt>Saturn return</dt>
        <dd>Saturn's return to its birth position, roughly every 29.5 years. The best-known transit and the most psychologically plausible.</dd>
        <dt>Precession</dt>
        <dd>The 25,800-year wobble of Earth's axis, which shifts the equinox point against the stars.</dd>
        <dt>Barnum effect</dt>
        <dd>The tendency to accept vague, broadly applicable statements as specifically and strikingly true of oneself.</dd>
      </dl>
    </div>

    <div class="frame reveal">
      <h2>Reading</h2>
      <ul class="biblio">
        <li><span class="hist">◆</span> <strong>Nicholas Campion</strong>, <em>A History of Western Astrology</em>, two volumes — the serious academic history, by a historian who takes the subject on its own terms.</li>
        <li><span class="hist">◆</span> <strong>Ptolemy</strong>, <em>Tetrabiblos</em> (c. 150 CE) — the foundational text of the whole Western tradition. Read the primary source.</li>
        <li><span class="hist">◆</span> <strong>Francesca Rochberg</strong>, <em>The Heavenly Writing</em> (2004) — Babylonian celestial divination, and how a science and a divination were once the same activity.</li>
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> <strong>Shawn Carlson</strong>, "A double-blind test of astrology", <em>Nature</em> 318 (1985) — six pages, read it yourself.</li>
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> <strong>Geoffrey Dean &amp; Ivan Kelly</strong>, "Is astrology relevant to consciousness and psi?" (2003) — the time-twins data and the meta-analytic case.</li>
        <li><span class="spec">◆</span> <strong>Richard Tarnas</strong>, <em>Cosmos and Psyche</em> (2006) — the most intellectually serious modern defence; correlational, unfalsifiable, and worth arguing with properly.</li>
        <li><span class="myth">◆</span> <strong>Liz Greene</strong>, <em>Saturn</em> (1976) — the psychological-astrology turn, and the clearest demonstration of what the symbolic language can do as a mirror.</li>
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> <strong>A planisphere and a pair of binoculars</strong> — genuinely. Costs less than two of the books above and will teach you more.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

